  "Dessert": {
    "Cyprus": [{"name": "Commandaria – Tsiakkas", "grape": "Xynisteri", "style": "Sweet", "region": "Cyprus", "appearance": "Amber-brown.", "nose": "Date, raisin, caramel, baking spice, orange peel.", "palate": "Full Body, Sweet and Silky. Ancient Cypriot dessert wine, dried fruit richness with refreshing lift.", "finish": "Long, spicy, warm and persistent.", "anecdote": "Costas Tsiakkas decided to make his own Commandaria after attempts to change production through his village cooperative bank failed. A 2004 article about Tokaji’s renewal had helped prompt his rethink, but securing grapes meant persuading growers worried about their contracts with larger wineries.\n\nHe rented cooperative space, bought a mobile cooling unit and two steel tanks, and committed to buying one grower’s entire crop from two vineyards. In the 2023 interview, he reported still working with that grower. He also stopped fortifying his Commandaria in 2008."}],
    "Italy": [{"name": "Recioto della Valpolicella – Dal Forno Romano", "grape": "Corvina Blend (Sweet)", "style": "Sweet", "region": "Veneto, IT", "appearance": "Inky ruby-purple, viscous legs.", "nose": "Dried black cherry, fig paste, dark chocolate, espresso, raisin, spice.", "palate": "Full Body, Sweet, Velvety Tannins. Extraordinarily concentrated dried fruit, rich and unctuous with balancing acidity.", "finish": "Very long, chocolate-espresso, warming and complex.", "anecdote": "Dal Forno Romano chose to keep its established sweet-wine style rather than alter it to meet appellation requirements. From the 2003 vintage, the wine’s characteristics were no longer considered compliant with Recioto della Valpolicella DOC rules, and the winery accepted IGT classification instead.\n\nThe estate named the resulting wine Vigna Seré, after a locality containing its vineyards, under the Veneto IGT designation. That change marks a documented turning point in the Recioto lineage of the estate Romano Dal Forno founded in 1983, when he began making wine from family vineyards."}, {"name": "Recioto di Soave \"I Capitelli\" – Anselmi", "grape": "Garganega (Sweet)", "style": "Sweet", "region": "Veneto, IT", "appearance": "Deep gold-amber.", "nose": "Dried apricot, honey, candied citrus, almond, saffron.", "palate": "Full Body, Sweet, Luscious Texture. Concentrated dried stone fruit, honeyed richness with fresh acidity lift.", "finish": "Long, honeyed, clean and persistent.", "anecdote": "Roberto Anselmi gradually closed his family’s wine-merchant operation, renting and then buying hillside vineyards. His father Almerino had founded the company in 1948 to bottle and sell purchased wines; Roberto chose instead to rebuild a vineyard property after his grandfather had been forced to sell his land amid wartime economic disruption.\n\nIn his professional biography, Roberto describes denser planting, less-productive Garganega selections and fewer bunches. He also names the passito I Capitelli among his wines, linking this cuvée to the vineyard-owning estate he built rather than the purchased-wine business he left behind."}, {"name": "Vin Santo \"Il Decano\" – Montepaldi", "grape": "Trebbiano/Malvasia (Sweet)", "style": "Sweet", "region": "Tuscany, IT", "appearance": "Deep amber.", "nose": "Dried fig, toasted walnut, caramel, honey, orange marmalade.", "palate": "Medium+ Body, Sweet. Raisined fruit with warm spice and nutty depth, balancing acidity.", "finish": "Long, nutty, toffee-like warmth.", "anecdote": "Emiliano Falsini returned to Villa Montepaldi’s cellar after studying at the estate and graduating in 1999. His professional return brought him back to the place where he had trained: a working agricultural estate that also carries out experimental and scientific research for the University of Florence.\n\nBy 2019, Falsini was documented as Montepaldi’s responsible oenologist, working alongside Nicola Menditto, the estate’s director and in-house oenologist. The former student had become part of its cellar team—not its sole identified maker—bringing his career back into the estate’s production work."}],
    "Portugal": [{"name": "Tawny Port \"Otima\" – Warre’s", "grape": "Douro Red Blend (Touriga Nacional Etc.)", "style": "Sweet", "region": "Douro, PT", "appearance": "Amber-tawny with golden rim.", "nose": "Caramel, dried fig, walnut, orange peel, toffee.", "palate": "Medium+ Body, Sweet, Smooth. Approachable tawny sweetness, dried fruit and nut with fresh acidity.", "finish": "Moderate-long, nutty, warming.", "anecdote": "Peter Symington selected wines from Warre’s Quinta da Cavadinha’s 1995 harvest, adding wine from Quinta do Retiro, for what became Otima Colheita 1995. That same year, his son Charles joined the family company and began an apprenticeship under Peter, then Head Winemaker.\n\nAfter Peter retired in 2009, Charles took responsibility for monitoring the selected 1995 wine as it continued ageing in seasoned oak casks. He later chose wines for bottling from 2018, carrying his father’s selection forward through a second generation of decisions within Warre’s Otima range."}, {"name": "Tawny Port – Quinta do Noval", "grape": "Douro Red Blend (Touriga Nacional Etc.)", "style": "Sweet", "region": "Douro, PT", "appearance": "Deep amber-mahogany.", "nose": "Raisin, roasted hazelnut, burnt caramel, dried orange, cinnamon.", "palate": "Full Body, Sweet, Silky. Complex aged tawny, layered dried fruit and nut with extraordinary persistence.", "finish": "Very long, spiced, lingering warmth.", "anecdote": "Christian Seely appointed António Agrellos technical director after becoming Quinta do Noval’s managing director in 1993. Following AXA Millésimes’ acquisition that year, the two undertook a joint programme of vineyard replanting and estate renovation. During the 1990s, around 100 hectares were replanted, with pruning methods adapted to individual parcels.\n\nThe rebuilding also reached beyond the vineyards: a new bottling line and warehouse at Alijó were completed in 1997. According to Noval’s history, this made the estate the first traditional Port shipper to centralise all its activities in the Douro Valley."}],
    "France": [{"name": "Les Carmes de Rieussec – Château Rieussec", "grape": "Sémillon/Sauvignon Blanc (Sweet Blend)", "style": "Sweet", "region": "Sauternes, FR", "appearance": "Sweet white Sauternes; exact release colour unconfirmed.", "nose": "Producer reference: a citrus-led aromatic profile.", "palate": "Sweet Sauternes. Carmes is distinct from the dry R de Rieussec.", "finish": "Exact release tasting and finish pending confirmation.", "anecdote": "Bertrand Roux, Rieussec’s cellar master, and estate director Mathieu Crosnier led the assessment of approximately sixty lots for the 2024 vintage. Rain throughout harvest had left Roux concerned before vinification; blending began in mid-January, with each participant scoring the wines from one to five.\n\nA score of one indicated Château Rieussec; three indicated Carmes de Rieussec. Collective blind tasting helped keep attachment to a lot’s production history from deciding its place. Two divisive lots, one Sauvignon and one Sémillon, remained under debate until late in the process."}]
  }
